# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke 15 opens: “Then drew near unto him all the publicans and sinners for to hear him” (15:1). Luke then says, “And the Pharisees and scribes murmured, saying, This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them” (15:2). What follows in chapter 15 is told to that scene. Chapter 16 begins, “And he said also unto his disciples” (16:1). Luke does not say where Jesus is at 16:1, whether the publicans and sinners are still near, or what the word “also” joins to.'))
A(('p', '2.2 Who hears. Luke names the disciples as the ones Jesus addresses at 16:1. He names the Pharisees later: “And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him” (16:14). Jesus then says to them, “Ye are they which justify yourselves before men; but God knoweth your hearts” (16:15). Luke does not say whether the Pharisees were present from 16:1 or came within hearing later, and he does not say that the parable was spoken to them.'))
A(('call', 'No Question Introduces This Parable',
   'No question or remark from a hearer is recorded before this story. It begins, “There was a certain rich man, which had a steward” (16:1), after the words “And he said also unto his disciples.” Luke does not say what Jesus is answering, and this study adds nothing to that.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. Luke has said what Jesus came to do: “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). Luke has also marked the road: “he stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem” (9:51), and “he went through the cities and villages, teaching, and journeying toward Jerusalem” (13:22). The story of the steward belongs to that road and to that teaching.'))
A(('p', '2.4 What follows. After the sayings of 16:9–13, Luke records the Pharisees’ response and Jesus’ words to them (16:14–15). The next verse reads, “The law and the prophets were until John: since that time the kingdom of God is preached, and every man presseth into it” (16:16). Luke does not say that this saying explains the steward, and the steward does not explain it. They are reported here as what stands next in the chapter.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives no statement of why Jesus told this parable. He gives no saying like the one he gives for the Lost Sheep, where the stories answer a stated murmuring in 15:2 by being told “unto them” (15:3). What Luke gives is the place in the chapter, the disciples (16:1), the sayings that follow (16:9–13), and the Pharisees who heard (16:14). The story is not a free-floating saying about money in general. It belongs to a stretch of teaching on the road to Jerusalem, and it is read there.'))
