# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The passage is read here in the order Luke gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The rich man and his steward (16:1). “And he said also unto his disciples, There was a certain rich man, which had a steward; and the same was accused unto him that he had wasted his goods.” The story begins with a certain rich man who has a steward. The steward “was accused” to the rich man that he had “wasted his goods.” The text reports an accusation. It does not say who accused him or whether the charge was true.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The account demanded (16:2). “And he called him, and said unto him, How is it that I hear this of thee? give an account of thy stewardship; for thou mayest be no longer steward.” The rich man calls the steward, asks a question about what he has heard, tells him to give an account, and says he may be no longer steward. The text does not give the steward’s answer to the question.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The steward’s reasoning (16:3–4). “Then the steward said within himself, What shall I do? for my lord taketh away from me the stewardship: I cannot dig; to beg I am ashamed. I am resolved what to do, that, when I am put out of the stewardship, they may receive me into their houses.” The steward speaks within himself. He asks what to do, names two things he cannot do, says he is resolved, and gives his purpose: that “they may receive me into their houses.” The text does not say who “they” are at this point.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The first debtor (16:5–6). “So he called every one of his lord’s debtors unto him, and said unto the first, How much owest thou unto my lord? And he said, An hundred measures of oil. And he said unto him, Take thy bill, and sit down quickly, and write fifty.” The steward calls every one of his lord’s debtors. He asks the first what he owes and is told “an hundred measures of oil.” He tells him to take his bill, sit down quickly and write fifty.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The second debtor (16:7). “Then said he to another, And how much owest thou? And he said, An hundred measures of wheat. And he said unto him, Take thy bill, and write fourscore.” The same action is told a second time with another debtor, a different good and a different sum. The text names two debtors, and says “every one” of the debtors were called. It does not say how many there were in all.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The commendation (16:8). “And the lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely: for the children of this world are in their generation wiser than the children of light.” The verse has two parts. First, “the lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely.” Second, a statement that begins with “for”: “the children of this world are in their generation wiser than the children of light.” The text does not say which lord is meant in 16:8, the rich man of the story or another. It does not mark a change of speaker between the two parts of the verse, and it does not say where the story stops and a saying begins. It names the man “the unjust steward” and says he “had done wisely,” and it does not reconcile the two words.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The sayings that follow (16:9–13). “And I say unto you, Make to yourselves friends of the mammon of unrighteousness; that, when ye fail, they may receive you into everlasting habitations.” This verse is marked as a speaker’s own word to “you.” Four more verses follow: 16:10 (faithful in the least, unjust in the least), 16:11 (the unrighteous mammon and the true riches), 16:12 (that which is another man’s and that which is your own) and 16:13 (no servant can serve two masters; “Ye cannot serve God and mammon”). They are set out word for word in Section 4.'))
A(('p', '3.8 The wording of 16:4 and 16:9. The steward’s purpose in 16:4 uses the words “they may receive me into their houses.” The saying of 16:9 uses the words “they may receive you into everlasting habitations.” The wording is set side by side here and no more is done with it. The text does not say that one verse explains the other.'))
