A(('p', '3.9 What the passage contains. The text names a certain rich man, a steward, an accusation, a demand for an account, the steward’s reasoning, his lord’s debtors, a hundred measures of oil and of wheat, a bill written down to fifty and to fourscore, a commendation, the children of this world and the children of light, the mammon of unrighteousness, everlasting habitations, the least and much, the unrighteous mammon and the true riches, another man’s and your own, two masters, God and mammon. The action moves from a charge, to a demanded account, to a plan, to two reduced bills, to a commendation, to a run of sayings.'))
A(('p', '3.10 What the passage leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the rich man is. Jesus does not say who the steward is, or who accused him, or whether the charge was true. Jesus does not say who the debtors are, or what a measure is, or how many debtors there were. Jesus does not say who “they” are in 16:4 or in 16:9. Jesus does not say what the mammon of unrighteousness is, what the everlasting habitations are, or what the true riches and that which is your own are. Jesus does not say who the children of this world and the children of light are. The text does not say what became of the steward after the commendation.'))

