A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because no identification is given, none is given here. A reader who says that the rich man is a certain person, that the debtors are a certain people, that the oil or the wheat is a certain thing, that the habitations are a certain place, or that the mammon of unrighteousness is a certain wealth is supplying what the text did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text does say. The text says that a rich man’s steward was accused of waste; that he was told to give an account; that he reasoned about what to do and gave his purpose; that he called his lord’s debtors and wrote down their bills; that “the lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely”; and that sayings follow on friends, faithfulness, what is another man’s and two masters. These statements are made by the passage itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'Luke 16:1 does not begin with a comparison to the kingdom of God. No opening comparison to the kingdom is stated for this story. This section therefore states only the meaning that arises from the story and the sayings as they stand, and it assigns no identity to any part of them.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. A rich man’s steward, accused of waste and told to give an account, reasons that he cannot dig and is ashamed to beg, and acts before the account is taken: he reduces the bills of his lord’s debtors. The lord commends him for having done wisely. Sayings follow about friends, faithfulness in the least and in much, what is another man’s, and two masters.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the story and the sayings communicate.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**An account is demanded.** The rich man says, “give an account of thy stewardship; for thou mayest be no longer steward” (16:2). The story turns on a reckoning that has been called for.',
    '**Foresight is shown.** The steward says, “I am resolved what to do” (16:4), and he acts at once. The lord’s commendation is for the doing: “because he had done wisely” (16:8).',
    '**Faithfulness is measured in what is least.** The saying is, “He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much: and he that is unjust in the least is unjust also in much” (16:10).',
    '**What is held is held as another’s.** The saying is, “if ye have not been faithful in that which is another man’s, who shall give you that which is your own?” (16:12).',
    '**Two masters cannot both be served.** The saying is, “No servant can serve two masters,” and it closes, “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (16:13).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Luke names are the disciples (16:1) and the Pharisees who heard and derided (16:14). They had heard what Jesus preached: “the kingdom of God” (4:43; 16:16). They were meant to understand that a stewardship can be called to account, that foresight in the face of a reckoning is a thing the lord commends, that faithfulness in little and in what is another’s is measured, and that service cannot be divided between two masters. Luke does not say that the rich man, the steward or the debtors in the story are these hearers or any other persons, and Jesus does not identify them.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the commendation that comes where none was expected. The steward is called “unjust,” and the lord says he “had done wisely.” The text sets both words side by side and does not reduce one to the other. The force is also in what the sayings do with the story: they speak of friends, of the least, of another man’s and of two masters, and they do not say how each saying stands to the story.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. An account can be demanded. Foresight is commended. Faithfulness is measured in the least. What is held is another’s. Two masters cannot both be served. These statements arise from the passage, and they do not say that the rich man, the steward, the debtors, the oil, the wheat, the mammon or the habitations represents anyone or anything. The saying about friends and everlasting habitations (16:9) is not made into a doctrine here: the text does not say who the friends are, who receives, or what the habitations are.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The steward stands among other passages in Luke and Matthew that use similar words. It is kept apart from them here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 15:11–32', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'The Lost Son and the Elder Brother. A father and two sons, a far country, a feast. It stands just before this chapter and is not an explanation of it. The word “wasted” is used of the younger son (15:13) and of the steward (16:1), and no connection between the two is stated.'],
    ['Luke 16:19–31', '“There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple”', 'The Rich Man and Lazarus. It follows in the same chapter, after 16:14–18. It opens with the same words, “a certain rich man,” and the text does not say that he is the rich man of 16:1. It stands in its own study.'],
    ['Luke 12:16–21', '“The ground of a certain rich man brought forth plentifully”', 'The Rich Fool. Another certain rich man, in another setting. He is not the rich man of 16:1.'],
    ['Luke 12:42–48', '“Who then is that faithful and wise steward”', 'Another steward, in the setting of Peter’s question. He is not the steward of 16:1, and the text does not join them.'],
    ['Matt 6:24', '“Ye cannot serve God and mammon”', 'The same closing words in another setting. They are not an explanation of Luke 16:13, and Luke 16:13 is not an explanation of them.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The steward is told in Luke on the road toward Jerusalem, “unto his disciples,” with the Pharisees named afterward (16:1, 14). The Lost Son is told in the scene of 15:1–2, among publicans and sinners and Pharisees and scribes. The Rich Man and Lazarus comes after 16:14–18. A shared word such as “rich man,” “steward” or “wasted” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The steward’s story has a rich man, a steward, two named debtors and “every one” of the lord’s debtors. The Lost Son has a father, two sons and a far country. The Rich Man and Lazarus has a rich man, a beggar, Abraham and the brothers. The Rich Fool has a rich man and his ground. No character is carried from one passage into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The steward moves from an accusation, to an account demanded, to a plan, to two reduced bills, to a commendation, to sayings. The Lost Son moves from a request, to a far country, to a return, to a feast, to an answer to the elder brother. The Rich Man and Lazarus moves from two lives, to two deaths, to a conversation. None of those movements is read into the others.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, “unjust” and “wisely” in 16:8 belong to this verse and are not read into the Rich Man or the Lost Son. Second, “wasted his goods” (16:1) is not the “wasted his substance” of 15:13, and the younger son is not the steward. Third, the words “children of light” appear elsewhere in other settings (John 12:36; Ephesians 5:8; 1 Thessalonians 5:5) and are not carried here to say who the children of light of 16:8 are. Fourth, the faithful and wise steward of Luke 12:42 is not read as the unjust steward of 16:8, and neither is read into the other.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the kingdom of God (4:43) and set His face toward Jerusalem (9:51). Luke names His hearers at 16:1 as His disciples, and he names the Pharisees at 16:14. The steward belongs to that ministry and to the road on which it was told.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The stewardship, the friends, the habitations and the masters of this passage belong to the Lord’s earthly teaching. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The rich man, the steward, the debtors, the mammon and the everlasting habitations are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The passage is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The accounting, the writing down of bills, the making of friends and the faithfulness in the least are actions and sayings in this passage, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The text does not say what “everlasting habitations” are, and no doctrine of salvation or of the place after death is drawn from them here. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the passage shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of stewardship.** A steward is answerable for what he holds (16:2). A reader learns that what he handles is entrusted and will be accounted for.',
    '**A lesson of foresight.** The steward is “resolved what to do” (16:4) and acts. A reader learns not to let a coming reckoning arrive without thought.',
    '**A lesson of faithfulness in little.** “He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much” (16:10). A reader learns that small trusts are not small tests.',
    '**A lesson of one master.** “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (16:13). A reader learns that service cannot be divided without cost.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The saying about friends and everlasting habitations is not turned into a lesson here, because the text does not say what it means. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'A rich man’s steward was accused of waste and told to give an account. He said within himself that he could not dig and was ashamed to beg, called his lord’s debtors, and wrote their bills down to fifty and to fourscore. The lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely. Sayings follow on friends and everlasting habitations, faithfulness in the least, what is another man’s, and two masters. Jesus names none of the parts and gives no explanation, and the text does not say whose voice speaks at the start of 16:8. The passage says that an account can be demanded, that foresight is commended, that faithfulness is measured in the least, and that God and mammon cannot both be served. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the rich man, the steward, the debtors, the habitations or the mammon are, or that makes the writing down of bills or the making of friends a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** an accusation of waste; an account demanded; the steward’s reasoning; two debtors and two reduced bills; the lord’s commendation; sayings on friends, the least, another man’s and two masters.',
    '**The meaning:** an account can be demanded; foresight is commended; faithfulness is measured in the least; God and mammon cannot both be served.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation naming any part; no identity for the rich man, the steward, the debtors, the mammon or the habitations; nothing carried in from Luke 15, the Rich Man and Lazarus, the Rich Fool or Matthew 6.',
    '**The present lesson:** stewardship, foresight, faithfulness in little and one master, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

