A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. A rich man’s steward was accused of waste and told to give an account. He reasoned that he could not dig and was ashamed to beg, called his lord’s debtors, and had their bills written down to fifty and to fourscore. The lord commended the unjust steward, because he had done wisely. Sayings follow on friends and everlasting habitations, faithfulness in the least, what is another man’s, and two masters. Jesus names none of the parts and gives no explanation. The passage says that an account can be demanded, that foresight is commended, that faithfulness is measured in the least, and that God and mammon cannot both be served.'))
